    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What battery does agricultural machinery use?</h3>
      <p>Tractors, combines and harvesters run diesel engines, so they need a high-CCA starting battery — typically 12V on smaller tractors and 24V on larger machines built from two 12V batteries in series. Group size follows the machine, commonly JIS N150/N200 or DIN88/DIN100 depending on origin, matched to the tray and CCA specification.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Why do batteries fail during the off-season on farms?</h3>
      <p>Agricultural machines sit idle for long periods between seasons, so batteries self-discharge while parked. A partially discharged battery left standing sulphates and loses capacity, and the first cold or heavy start of the new season is then too much for it. A maintenance routine or a charger during storage prevents this.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Is AGM better than flooded for farm equipment?</h3>
      <p>AGM handles vibration and dust better than vented flooded designs and is sealed, which helps on rough fields and sloping ground. But flooded thick-plate batteries remain the standard, lower-cost choice where the duty is simple starting. The choice is conditional on the machine's duty and charging system.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How should I store a tractor battery over the off-season?</h3>
      <p>Keep it charged — a fully charged battery self-discharges more slowly and is far less prone to sulphation. Either disconnect and trickle-charge periodically, or use a maintenance charger during storage, and store the battery in a cool, dry place.</p>
    </div>

